#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152005 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "5fe8dafe-b03c-54b6-a737-6572f59b97f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6995" :question/problem "![](images/11/1191de029d927394dcc587f9c432a2d7cf4cec136e6d6ebbe62799de7b4f9b3e.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-1,1] \\times [0,1],$ and\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D |x-y| \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}|x-y|, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ |x-y|, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}|x-y|, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/d9/d9ca0bfcfde5839033279cb09f8ccece7c59a5b8f8b24eaae3697fd5358dc6b3.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $|x-y|$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D |x-y| \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function I. This functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,\\backslash D.$ For example, $(x,y) = (1,0) \\in R\\,\\backslash\\, D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\n- Functions II and III equal $|x-y|$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152006 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "80f011ae-c472-5dfa-8303-70396ecf51fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6996" :question/problem "![](images/73/7325a35720157e2331ade1b9abd042f90030361c1560dd3bf98de08cc495e4c4.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n![](images/bf/bf3c5a6d11a579553b45d0b0c0075a527953e4ffe825427d53839dbcd8e16e7d.svg)\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-2,2] \\times [-2,1]$\n\nII. $R=[-2,1] \\times [-2,2]$\n\nIII. $R=[-1,1] \\times [-1,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n![](images/3d/3dfc3848c566e7648a776c35b9de046a2d90904d5a6d1779643d696ebbe4f307.svg)\n\nthen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D (x^2-y^2) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II cover the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Rectangle III does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152007 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/id #uuid "fe8eb478-6830-55bc-84c6-668924daaefc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6997" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $x^2+y^2=9.$ If \n![](images/e8/e83d9c7f3dd0397ea9a748ef4d6ceb15ff14b0f72166767924a93ef41b796a5a.svg) \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{\\large R} \\cos(x-y) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{\\large D} f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A,\\quad$ where $R=[-4,4] \\times [-4,4]$\n\nII. $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{\\large D} \\cos(x-y) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{\\large R} f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A,\\quad$ where $R=[-2,2] \\times [-2,2]$\n\nIII. $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{\\large D} \\cos(x-y) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{\\large R} f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A,\\quad$ where $R=[-3,3] \\times [-3,3]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/3a/3aab7a115fe72d3796be28c66f4ce9ae2c15dc56e09efd98e5ad9a45edebd704.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n![](images/c5/c598ed6ff9209d8456e36df1b0a140ddb43150a5c3c7060232603be0ae4598f3.svg)\n\nthen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{\\large D} \\cos(x-y) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{\\large R} f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement II is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "264cf4e2-8ed4-58f5-90ff-ad6c657b4128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444885} {:answer/id #uuid "74756762-e410-57d4-95d6-39a30bfc37b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444887} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c2f725-8315-5690-b52c-5a28778570c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444891} {:answer/id #uuid "b82083db-b8de-5a77-af8d-e0d8604e5a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444892} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd8a44b-f98b-5eb8-bbd1-c98d25b54697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "264cf4e2-8ed4-58f5-90ff-ad6c657b4128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444885} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c6b02fe-a198-551b-8207-64840a262a3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b739dd6-c1ea-5425-a4f3-ff629b5d83cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-129653" :question/problem "![](images/55/555eec8e88dd658903e19b8779f85598f57126b054d5058b662824f09de0f6cb.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-x)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]$\n\nII. $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,2]$\n\nIII. $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,0]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-x)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and III do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle II covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265765 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cf8d0c2-b55b-54c8-80e6-218235c3d5cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263379} {:answer/id #uuid "4e97e4a9-6185-5e4d-910d-c538b1cb2ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263682} {:answer/id #uuid "98309dde-974d-523a-9d07-628f7f8605d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264308} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e6e5a3-e7aa-5e4c-885b-491a4f477364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265160} {:answer/id #uuid "f806378f-86bd-5e52-a34e-5e4c3e4a0105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6e6e5a3-e7aa-5e4c-885b-491a4f477364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265160} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4f91e8d-5ee1-5973-b445-d68045b24cdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16d77304-e003-57b3-942e-c991453c4142" :question/math-academy-id "q-129658" :question/problem "![](images/b9/b946843e712c6294e261cb3015b02bc065cd265763805cf046009a2db0d11b83.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-1,3]\\times [-2,1]$\n\nII. $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n\nIII. $R=[-2,3]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle III covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380838 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4b2bc5c-f10b-5b15-9c53-5b71c501c0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380840} {:answer/id #uuid "550e22a7-8d98-5669-a03f-2617ea80ed7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380841} {:answer/id #uuid "082de418-b4f7-599c-972c-ae9a1fee0643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380842} {:answer/id #uuid "d690c9d1-dc38-5277-823b-6228cf42526e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380843} {:answer/id #uuid "a25a7123-e2cd-58ca-857d-079bc6e0ce4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "550e22a7-8d98-5669-a03f-2617ea80ed7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380841} :answer-field/id #uuid "afa4a9d2-1409-5e7a-9148-465b1c07f58d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d32f310d-7c80-57bd-8ae7-821e89abc170" :question/math-academy-id "q-129901" :question/problem "![](images/e2/e2ded513c72c8029b7d2909d2e3b37cbd659c4ba60a7be72c81379626a7a1234.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-1,2]\\times [-1,3],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/77/77c8e1b1aacd29c6d69aa3c641c1641f43ff2aa1467dc713d417641f8b76b066.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal ${e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions II and III. Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,3)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function I equals ${e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cefff60-8b5a-5e6b-9e52-f35453d404f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263940} {:answer/id #uuid "72297846-23e3-55fb-b49d-2016ee961011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263988} {:answer/id #uuid "b17137ba-a39f-5351-bf5d-ac9b76a0c325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264527} {:answer/id #uuid "c573ea7b-2439-51c4-b299-30120bf6c0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264732} {:answer/id #uuid "f1b5de7f-f5db-5245-8a64-60266e3ac04d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1b5de7f-f5db-5245-8a64-60266e3ac04d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265124} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ed46d99-55db-5652-860e-3ffa744aad41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2768639-7882-5bba-9579-987ae66a0169" :question/math-academy-id "q-129904" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f8cb975b622bfe420d9418587293e83de60c297019f15e1b379bc118e079d35d.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/89/8944afb608465e49c7ae253f8e2518aa3d43b1c6e121dd91178ca1a67116a508.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions I and III. Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function II equals $3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb4515cd-9e47-5ff7-b9db-80d4caff3722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380847} {:answer/id #uuid "9fd2179a-393c-5d9d-935b-1e1aabd85a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380848} {:answer/id #uuid "294b19d4-4da4-5a2d-964c-fb0115fffed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380849} {:answer/id #uuid "5d683d66-22db-5f8a-9a0c-c12b9c67e331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380850} {:answer/id #uuid "fc2be26d-1186-5a98-9d33-d6449a993930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d683d66-22db-5f8a-9a0c-c12b9c67e331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380850} :answer-field/id #uuid "e22d5e18-f2d7-5ad6-beaf-4cf2d9f87028" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a021db95-99b8-59d4-be5a-36dd8114ebe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-129907" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.$ If $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ y{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/38/38c44f897999039f88df2c3e549a1229ec9f9a979bbc83dfe1a5d7279701450d.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $y{e}^{x+y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and II equal $y{e}^{x+y}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function III. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(3,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ee5e9b6-3e97-56e9-853f-9d72f1154481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345622} {:answer/id #uuid "23faa8ae-d9f3-5f57-b21e-6362df73adc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345682} {:answer/id #uuid "3cfde730-bed7-5b3c-a445-7ce2d3fb6c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345866} {:answer/id #uuid "47f8e205-cafd-57bb-b6b4-2dd8d3754108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345968} {:answer/id #uuid "dcee67be-ab48-5537-8451-b5d9a776340f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47f8e205-cafd-57bb-b6b4-2dd8d3754108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345968} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a852fd7-7eb9-5d40-a916-7e4688b70024" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e648a84-4fc0-5e89-9abf-3bfad9d2cf64" :question/math-academy-id "q-80266" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,1]$\n\nII. $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,2]$\n\nIII. $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/73/736575dceb6969f8b45e4038f702ebc84347ab66a1a8e5ea5103840ddd61ba31.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangle I does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangles II and III cover the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "396e3f27-e24a-553a-9d76-87b06e254ca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436030} {:answer/id #uuid "4facf5d2-11cf-5ead-aac1-8f2480139664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436031} {:answer/id #uuid "ae28b501-c23e-599b-8e7e-1db8502ac7b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436034} {:answer/id #uuid "cf5cd3c6-1bfc-586c-9f3c-4788cef42fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436035} {:answer/id #uuid "d970f7e7-4967-5568-882e-bad44890c0c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae28b501-c23e-599b-8e7e-1db8502ac7b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436034} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee7f980b-3738-5f95-89d8-bd69eb485ba5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34094a62-379f-5750-81e2-774654924818" :question/math-academy-id "q-80505" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]$\n\nII. $R=[-1,1]\\times [0,1]$\n\nIII. $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/c5/c50e0e01d432b1db736fad09dd5f5b82286e981d88586e848d9b57c8a9910eb0.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangle II does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangles I and III covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382400 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b312bca-7b4b-56e2-8482-c0ca71388a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382402} {:answer/id #uuid "1dfa578f-8811-57c8-9b85-22eaebad9de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382403} {:answer/id #uuid "5a61b453-e0d2-5d28-805b-f83953fedfaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382404} {:answer/id #uuid "36be8ca1-404b-52e1-9fa2-b0ea626ad08d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382405} {:answer/id #uuid "023cfac3-5fc6-5252-b244-d062063af8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b312bca-7b4b-56e2-8482-c0ca71388a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382402} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b116226-ab8b-5ce9-ac44-338ae9414626" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47830ab2-2bae-52c6-8c9c-df89a69a6369" :question/math-academy-id "q-80536" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∬}_{R}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,2]$\n\nII. ${∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$\n\nIII. ${∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-3,3]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/5c/5c5dbc673c2c6a9bd0a4f06223133e21e2dcbbdbaa2dfe2de2b29d8824dd821e.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statements II and III are true. In both cases, the rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "415bb700-ad37-5b82-8f47-9fbd2f758c2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345908} {:answer/id #uuid "957eeaba-b453-56e9-83a4-66194e19c6c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346655} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c395b8-00cc-5a0d-8bfb-d4a91c203fff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346806} {:answer/id #uuid "dd801d1a-f53a-5e1e-a862-90c86397a850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347297} {:answer/id #uuid "f8290031-63e9-596d-b94a-459649d47293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd801d1a-f53a-5e1e-a862-90c86397a850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347297} :answer-field/id #uuid "5539bcfd-a712-5e1a-a393-3af3955fb0d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b7813ca-41d9-58f5-aaf1-c07ba10ad235" :question/math-academy-id "q-80540" :question/problem "![](images/50/506a298041a875549ec60c5cf99ebdbeb5ca4b5717037083fa6ca196fce27279.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [0,3],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{2x+y}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ \\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/d2/d2b5c55b15f594680e8f299899d690cd1d92baa1ebef31ce5c79af52afec631e.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\frac{2x+y}{y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{2x+y}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function II. Since $D∖R$ is empty, the function $f(x,y)$ will be identical to zero for all points in $R,$ including $D.$ But $\\frac{2x+y}{y}$ is not zero, for example, at $(x,y)=(0,2)\\in D.$\n\n- Functions I and III equals $\\frac{2x+y}{y}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444897 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02227aca-c031-59fd-93c4-2fafbc07e7f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444884} {:answer/id #uuid "6f3c18f9-58fa-5eac-9421-703a0fbb6877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444886} {:answer/id #uuid "78cab8ed-0dae-5d25-9514-1cf1de27f303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444888} {:answer/id #uuid "8b2af876-19a0-51e0-afde-f9a9bf4046f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444889} {:answer/id #uuid "8df20bc4-f189-5c1c-a2c5-e4c7ac3802cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b2af876-19a0-51e0-afde-f9a9bf4046f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444889} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebe09a19-f78b-5d26-9864-3cc9c0f88600" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fac0df1b-f9cc-511c-8006-433930d0444f" :question/math-academy-id "q-80568" :question/problem "![](images/12/12acb33aa174f1c174a538a89881dd19245f733c6b39df37f9ebbdc1f86442d5.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-3,1]\\times [-1,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({2}^{x}-y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x}-y, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x}-y, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x}-y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/56/56f6557f98674b0f651a781e6226ba9d207273e30136e3396c3aabcf91a64024.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal ${2}^{x}-y$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({2}^{x}-y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions I and II. Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,0)\\in R∖D$, but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function III equals ${2}^{x}-y$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354529 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7164d4-1452-58ed-9be7-815fab7aa332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351668} {:answer/id #uuid "0e762721-ea6b-5edb-97a3-8c31e2fc66f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351688} {:answer/id #uuid "389ccd71-cb5f-5580-bb15-b89e8ab9ecdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352090} {:answer/id #uuid "98ab4ecf-2247-5a6f-81e8-9304719bf363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352879} {:answer/id #uuid "baf17d0c-88b3-5cd6-8160-6f4dac88865f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98ab4ecf-2247-5a6f-81e8-9304719bf363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352879} :answer-field/id #uuid "498e42e0-1fd5-563a-84b3-bcd8608d5ea0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63f88f2b-5634-54ed-8998-5ec059a5e869" :question/math-academy-id "q-86861" :question/problem "![](images/53/534f6ef0206bd4281320968c8a60b88f7f237e48c31e3d10c1080ddb69b9f56f.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-1,2]\\times [0,1]$\n\nII. $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n\nIII. $R=[-2,2]\\times [0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and III do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle II covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "657bd7d3-dd1c-5d1e-a2e2-e5779123f72b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407284} {:answer/id #uuid "784f60d8-744f-5a20-8590-4309ac0a7fb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407286} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac4f900-28cf-5d47-aca1-6ede50a776f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407287} {:answer/id #uuid "c63263fe-1e9f-59ea-a5b6-858826246c29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407289} {:answer/id #uuid "fdc6305c-bf3a-5c13-af4f-f56b112873c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "784f60d8-744f-5a20-8590-4309ac0a7fb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407286} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e76b06d-2177-5f41-ae22-04ed4efb0ed9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed965e5b-f0df-5c0f-9c7c-0cb7a433c067" :question/math-academy-id "q-86866" :question/problem "![](images/51/51706be38cc1c72079fe702c899b575ea53ba4d85e7990a6aa81f20dc8da4de3.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true? \n\nI. ${∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [0,3]$\n\nII. ${∬}_{R}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-5,5]\\times [0,3]$\n\nIII. ${∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement II is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354530 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30460c98-d31d-594b-8b7c-68d7db0cafdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352009} {:answer/id #uuid "6bf219a0-b95d-5611-b2dd-adafaf4e5378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352522} {:answer/id #uuid "7069aab4-27eb-5e1a-9128-7fc186cc5530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352554} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf4a37e-8cd1-55b4-bad5-44ffa6ae4754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352794} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a780f1-4dce-565c-9007-c2e4dc9f03a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3a780f1-4dce-565c-9007-c2e4dc9f03a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353698} :answer-field/id #uuid "04ab3e40-1b7b-5725-a424-443b8aa544da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "020f4792-4587-5aa1-be12-a9175f71b2e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-86867" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$\n\nII. ${∬}_{R}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$\n\nIII. ${∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/62/62925844de27c9731b181f17a1bdd912bfa3265176bd62ebda425d182ae8d9e8.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The rectangle $R$ does not completely cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}]}
